      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Impact on integrated SRHR: Supportive\n Partial\n Punitive\nLaws and policies\nPeople living with HIV\nAre there laws or policies that:\nCriminalize transmission, exposure or non-\ndisclosure of HIV\nYes\nRestrict entry, stay and residence of people\nliving with HIV\nYes\nProtect against discrimination on basis of HIV\nstatus\nND\nAllow HIV self-testing\nNo\nMake oral pre-exposure prophylaxis for HIV\n(PrEP) available\nYes\n19\n20\n21\n22\n23\nSRHR and gender-based violence\nAre there laws or policies that:\nAllow safe abortion\n- To save a woman's life\nYes\n- To preserve a woman's physical health\nNo\n- To preserve a woman",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Summarises Kuwait's laws and policies affecting SRHR, showing for example that HIV transmission is criminalized, entry for people with HIV is restricted, PrEP is available but self-testing is not allowed. It also records that abortion is allowed only to save a woman's life or for foetal impairment, spousal consent is required for married women's SRH services, and same-sex behaviour, sex work and drug offences carry punitive laws.",
